          Executive Summary: Homeowners Insurance Trends
        </div>
        <ul class="callout-list">
          <li><strong>National Average Premium:</strong> The average American homeowner pays <strong>$2,151 per year</strong> for a standard policy with $350,000 in dwelling coverage. However, coastal and severe-weather states (Florida, Louisiana, Texas, Oklahoma) average between $3,800 and $5,600 annually.</li>
          <li><strong>Replacement Cost &ne; Market Value:</strong> Never insure your home based on what you bought it for or its Zillow estimate. Real estate market value includes land, school district premiums, and location demand; insurance only pays for building materials and construction labor to rebuild the physical structure.</li>
          <li><strong>Watch Out for ACV Roof Endorsements:</strong> Insurers are increasingly shifting aging roofs (10–15+ years) from Replacement Cost Value (RCV) to Actual Cash Value (ACV), exposing homeowners to thousands in depreciation deductions after hail or windstorms.</li>
        </ul>
