      <p class="lead-text">
        Homeowners insurance rates have experienced substantial upward adjustments as construction material inflation, skilled labor shortages, and concentrated convective storms alter insurer loss ratios. Understanding the specific mechanics of your HO-3 or HO-5 policy is critical to safeguarding your family's largest physical asset.
      </p>

      <h2>The Six Standard Coverages Inside Every HO-3 Policy</h2>
      <p>
        The standard homeowner policy in the US is the ISO HO-3 (Special Form). It divides your protection into six clearly defined coverage buckets:
      </p>

      <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Coverage Section</th>
              <th>What It Covers</th>
              <th>Standard Policy Formula</th>
              <th>Typical Limit ($350k Home)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Coverage A: Dwelling</strong></td>
              <td>The physical structure: framing, roof, drywall, foundation, built-in cabinetry</td>
              <td>100% of estimated replacement cost</td>
              <td>$350,000</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Coverage B: Other Structures</strong></td>
              <td>Detached garages, tool sheds, perimeter fences, gazebos, driveways</td>
              <td>10% of Coverage A</td>
              <td>$35,000</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Coverage C: Personal Property</strong></td>
              <td>Furniture, electronics, clothes, appliances (worldwide protection)</td>
              <td>50% to 70% of Coverage A</td>
              <td>$175,000 – $245,000</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Coverage D: Loss of Use</strong></td>
              <td>Additional living expenses (hotel, rental, dining) while your home is uninhabitable</td>
              <td>20% to 30% of Coverage A</td>
              <td>$70,000 – $105,000</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Coverage E: Personal Liability</strong></td>
              <td>Lawsuits for bodily injury or property damage you or family members cause to others</td>
              <td>Selected limit ($100k min; $300k–$500k standard)</td>
              <td>$300,000 – $500,000</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Coverage F: Medical Payments</strong></td>
              <td>Minor medical bills for guests injured on your property regardless of legal fault</td>
              <td>Fixed limit ($1,000 to $5,000 per person)</td>
              <td>$5,000</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
